{
  "image_name": "739e3c9ab27616501b44d108849ffb14.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares several mainstream crystalline-silicon solar-cell architectures by showing simplified cross-sections of their layer stacks and contact schemes. Across the panels, the main message is how different designs combine front texturing, passivation layers, doped regions, and metal contacts to reduce recombination while still extracting current.\n\nThe first group shows p-type concepts such as Al-BSF, PERC, and p-PERT. These use a textured front with a silicon nitride coating and differ mainly at the rear: the older Al-BSF relies on an aluminum back-surface field, while PERC and p-PERT introduce rear dielectric passivation such as Al2O3/SiNx and localized contacts, indicating improved surface passivation and contact control.\n\nThe second group shows n-type concepts including n-PERT and two forms of TOPCon. These include boron emitters, phosphorus back-surface fields, and in TOPCon a very thin tunnel oxide plus doped polysilicon rear contact, emphasizing selective, passivated carrier extraction.\n\nThe last group shows heterojunction and back-contact approaches. SHJ uses thin amorphous-silicon layers and transparent conductive oxide, while SHJ-IBC and IBC move contacts to the rear. Overall, the figure highlights a progression from simpler full-area contacts toward more strongly passivated, selective, and rear-contacted cell designs.",
